the protesters have feared the security  services storming their camp for weeks  and it started in the early morning unit  of the notoriously brutal rapid support  forces the RSF began clearing part of  the protest square but it rapidly  escalated into a full-on assault the  protesters scattered through the streets  of Khartoum with live rounds whistling  around them dozens have been killed or  injured in the clearance operation it's  unclear why it started but it's brought  chaos to the capital once again the  anti-government demonstrators set fires  and blocked roads and a bridge across  the Nile  they're promising to rise up against the  military and to attempt to take back  control hundreds of thousands have been  camped outside Sudan's military  headquarters for weeks  they force the removal of dictator Omar  al-bashir but talks on a transfer of  power from the military have stalled a  clear-out operation was always a  possibility although only weeks ago the  head of the military told me the  protesters would be safe sorrowful oh  man he my heart my god don't don't don't  yeah the RSF are now deployed across  Khartoum cars are being randomly stopped  drivers and passengers beaten it's  feared this is the start of a permanent  crackdown by at least part of the  security forces the leaders of the  protest movement are calling on people  to take to the streets across the  country and again in huge numbers the  military have said it still wants to  continue talks or whatever trust there  was between the two sides  we'll have all but disappeared now mr.  Ramsay Sky News
 

